# Proposal: a local, provenance-first biomedical image review workspace

**Stage:** I-05-LUNA-CLOCK-FRESH-R001-treatment-research-a001 · research proposal · 2026-10-06  
**Purpose:** plan an incremental desktop research tool for six people exchanging project bundles through shared storage. It is not a diagnostic product.

## Recommendation

Build a Python desktop application around a narrow, testable image-reading boundary: OME-TIFF through `tifffile`’s read-only Zarr interface and local OME-Zarr 0.4 stores through Zarr v2. Keep image samples immutable. Persist project metadata and annotation edits in an application-owned SQLite database; use the viewer only to display pixels and capture annotation gestures. Treat the project’s coordinate transform and data provenance as first-class records owned by the application, not implicit viewer state.

Use napari’s Shapes/Points/Labels mechanisms as the first UI prototype, behind a small adapter. The pinned Shapes source documents n-dimensional geometry, shape kinds including polygon/path, features, affine transforms, and scale/translation/shear. That is a useful implementation precedent, but neither its layer model nor its issue history establishes correct project persistence, undo semantics, registration, or exports for this proposed application. If an integration spike cannot reliably map screen positions back to named image axes and persist edit commands, use a custom Qt/VisPy canvas while retaining the same image readers, coordinate contract, and project database. A custom canvas is more work; a viewer widget has less control over its transform/event lifecycle. This is a bounded decision to test, not a proven performance or compatibility result.

The recommended reader proof-of-concept pin is `tifffile 2024.9.20` with `zarr 2.18.3`, a pair the tifffile release notes name among its tested requirements. Its `aszarr=True` example performs a selected tile read and exposes Dask chunks; its OME-TIFF notes also identify unsupported cases, including multi-file pyramidal OME-TIFF. The OME-NGFF 0.4 edition is the chosen interchange boundary for local OME-Zarr. napari `v0.5.6` is the proposed UI-spike pin, not an approved product lock. No complete application dependency set has been installed, tested, or shown compatible here. Pin the final Python/Qt/NumPy/decoder stack only after building the cross-platform spike and fixture matrix.

## Evidence, inference, choices, and limits

**External facts.** OME-TIFF maps IFD planes using OME-XML `DimensionOrder` and `TiffData`; the explicit IFD mapping may override the default plane order. Its dataset may span files with UUID references [S2]. OME-NGFF 0.4 uses Zarr v2, permits 2–5 axes, defines named axes and scale/translation mappings per resolution level, and constrains multiscale axis order to time, channel, then spatial axes [S1]. Its image-label representation is an integer-valued segmentation mask associated with a source image and multiscale data; it does not encode the proposed full points/polygons annotation contract [S1]. `tifffile 2024.9.20` documents TIFF/BigTIFF/OME-TIFF support and selected reads through a Zarr store, while explicitly documenting an implemented subset and format exclusions [S3]. SQLite describes transaction atomicity under stated filesystem/OS assumptions, with its cited atomic-commit article specifically explaining rollback-journal mode and referring WAL users to a different mechanism [S9].

**Engineering inferences.** Chunk- or tile-backed selection can avoid allocating another entire decoded array if the application never calls a whole-array conversion, but the component documentation is not a 3 GB viewer benchmark. TIFF strip/tile layout and codec can still make small selections expensive. A matrix of known files/codecs, bounded cache, cancellation, and peak-memory measurements is required. Named axes and explicit transform data reduce the chance that annotations silently drift when a view or derived array changes; they do not make ambiguous source metadata true. A checksum detects byte changes, not scientific provenance or authenticity.

**Product choices.** Store source array coordinates and immutable asset identity with each annotation. Define integer index 0 as the center of the first sample; continuous geometry uses that same center-based coordinate system. For supported axes, map sample coordinates to physical coordinates as `p = translation + scale × index`, in named-axis order and with units. Keep an `origin_state` of `measured`, `user_corrected`, `assumed_local_zero`, or `unknown`; do not present assumed zero as a calibrated absolute origin. Persist matrices and values as IEEE float64 (JSON numeric values plus a canonical binary/hash representation for identity checks); reject NaN/infinite transform parameters. The exact array-axis order and the transform’s named input/output axes are stored, never guessed from display order.

**Proposed validation.** Every performance, crash-recovery, compatibility, input/output, keyboard, and cross-machine behavior below remains unverified for a complete application. `witnesses.json` distinguishes the one candidate-executed arithmetic check from proposed/UNEXECUTED application checks.

## Component choices and independent precedents

1. **Chunked image access and durable format semantics — OME-TIFF/OME-NGFF plus tifffile.** These are independently useful mechanisms, not duplicate whole-product viewers. The OME specifications define multidimensional axes, physical units, resolution transforms, and file-plane mapping; tifffile is an implementation that can expose TIFF-backed array selections without asking the UI to own decoding. The OME-TIFF specification’s `DimensionOrder` examples show why page number cannot be treated as `Z` or `T` without consulting XML. The tagged tifffile README shows `imread(..., aszarr=True)`, a selected region read, and Dask chunking, but says compressed decoding may require `imagecodecs`, limits its TIFF subset, and says multi-file pyramidal OME-TIFF is not supported. Consequently, “tifffile reads TIFF” is not a format-compatibility promise. Pin the reader dependencies, then support only the combinations that pass the proposed fixture suite.

2. **Interactive multidimensional annotation — napari `Shapes` implementation.** The source at tag `v0.5.6` documents 2D/n-dimensional shape vertices, paths and polygons, annotation features/properties, layer transforms, and out-of-core caching limited in that source to Dask arrays [S4]. This is a useful interaction precedent independent of tifffile/OME storage: it contributes geometry editing, selection, and layer-transform concepts. Do not make napari’s own arrays or layer serialization the durable project format. Keep exact geometry, scopes, UUIDs, commands, and source binding in our database; mirror edits into the viewer and round-trip them through a test adapter.

3. **Recoverable project state — SQLite.** SQLite transactions are a separate persistence mechanism; the atomic-commit documentation explains the rollback journal’s recovery logic and its assumptions about locks, sector writes, deletes, and flushes [S9]. Store project records in a single SQLite database using a tested rollback-journal configuration for a single active writer. The application must still test its target filesystems. This does not justify editing a live database from multiple workstations over arbitrary network shares.

These precedents are independent because they address distinct responsibilities and have separate implementation histories: file-backed sample selection/metadata (OME and tifffile), annotation geometry/view interaction (napari), and transactional project persistence (SQLite). No one component establishes end-to-end correctness.

## Incremental workflow and minimum feature contract

1. **Create project and import.** Create a directory project with `project.sqlite`, a versioned manifest, optional `derived/`, and temporary `.incoming/` area. Import by default as a read-only external reference; never open a source for writing. An optional “copy into bundle” mode copies original bytes to a staging file, checks size and streaming SHA-256, then installs the copy and commits its asset record. Import progress reports bytes/planes; cancel removes or quarantines the staging file and leaves no committed asset. Save the original metadata block verbatim, parsed metadata, reader/version, import time, original filename, and any user corrections.
2. **Inspect.** Show source path and availability, byte size, content hash and whether hashing is complete, format/reader and decoder version, series, dtype, array shape, named axes/order, channel names, source calibration and units, origin status, and warnings. Display original metadata beside any normalized/corrected mapping. Unknown axes or inconsistent scale/shape are visibly unresolved; do not silently select an axis interpretation.
3. **View and navigate.** Present image, channel visibility/color, intensity mapping, slice, time index, and pixel/physical coordinate readout. Display mapping (per-channel window/level, LUT, gamma, channel combination) is a `ViewState`; it does not mutate source samples. Keep source and derived layers visibly labeled. Decode selected planes/chunks asynchronously; use a bounded cache (initial design setting: 512 MiB, subject to measurement), coalesce duplicate reads, show progress for long scans, and check cancellation between I/O/decode units. Optional GPU accelerates display only; it is not required for correctness.
4. **Annotate.** Support points, 2D polygon/path geometry, and raster masks. An annotation has a stable UUID, type, geometry, named `spatial_axes`, coordinate basis (`source_index`, `physical`, or `derived_index`), selected channel/time/slice scope, author, created/edited timestamps, style ID, free-text fields, and asset/transform reference. Scope is explicit: a 2D slice annotation fixes the omitted spatial axis and chosen time/channel; a volume annotation uses 3D geometry; an acquisition-wide mark states which time/channel selectors are `all`. Do not infer that a polygon spans z or time because it is visible in one view. Reusable styles are versioned project records with stroke/fill/width/opacity and label defaults. Store one undo/redo command per user edit (and selection changes only if they affect data); commit command and resulting state in one DB transaction. Preserve the command log across reopen so a saved edit can be undone after reopening.
5. **Save, close, reopen.** Annotation and view-state edits commit transactionally. Reopening validates schema version, database integrity, source identity, and transform invertibility; loads the last committed project snapshot, view state, and commands. If a linked file is missing, offer relocation by path, filename/size, then full hash verification. If bytes differ, keep the annotation bound to its prior hash and require the user to rebind/fork or cancel; never silently attach it to new pixels. Cross-machine validation uses a second workstation or clean profile, not a claim based on opening in the same process.
6. **Export.** Generate (a) `annotations.csv` plus a JSON sidecar and (b) a transparent rendered overlay for an explicitly selected image/time/channel/slice/view. Also generate a small, bounded crop as a new OME-TIFF derivative. A crop is a geometric data operation: store source asset/hash, axis-aligned ROI, resulting origin/transform, sample dtype, output hash, reader/writer versions, and operation ID. The default crop copies samples without resampling; any resample requires a separate confirmation and a recorded operation with interpolation, output dtype, parameters, and software pin. Keep exports in a staging area until each is closed, reopened, and checked; a failed export does not update its final path or the project’s committed export record.

## Data identity, transforms, and export meaning

An `Asset` row should have a random internal ID plus content SHA-256, byte length, source URI/path, external-vs-copied mode, format/series, dtype/shape, raw metadata bytes or lossless text, parsed metadata, axis list, original-axis order, reader pin, and calibration record. A hash is stronger than a path or timestamp but requires a cancellable sequential read for large files. Until verified, identify the asset as `hash_pending` and label exports provisional; recheck size/hash before a final export. For reference mode, a path relocation must compare full content identity before reusing annotations. For copied mode, verify the copied file against the source hash before marking import complete.

`Transform` records name the source and target spaces and axes, units, matrix, convention (`index0_is_sample_center`), and provenance (reader metadata, user correction, or derived operation). For a 2D/3D affine, use an explicit homogeneous matrix and its inverse. Coordinate order is written in the record and export; never conflate displayed `(row,column)` with physical `(x,y)`. The viewer’s screen/canvas transform and current slice are transient: invert them to data coordinates, then use the persisted image transform for physical values. Channel labels are selectors/categories, not spatial coordinates. Time uses its own axis unit or index and is never multiplied by a spatial scale.

OME-NGFF 0.4 supports scale/translation transform metadata, not the arbitrary affine this application may need for a corrected orientation or later geometric operation [S1]. If an image requires a shear, rotation, nonorthogonal stack, nonlinear warp, or ambiguous orientation, keep its raw metadata and choose one of: (1) user supplies a reviewed invertible mapping and the project stores it outside NGFF’s native mapping; (2) derive/resample to an explicitly new image and export annotations in that derivative’s axes; or (3) reject annotation import while preserving the project/import report. Never pretend this mapping round-trips through NGFF 0.4 without loss. Export source-index annotations and transform sidecar even when physical placement is unknown.

The CSV uses one row per annotation, stable annotation and asset IDs, source SHA-256, source series, geometry type, named axes, geometry as compact JSON arrays of named coordinates, explicit selectors (including `all`), coordinate basis, units, style ID, and transform ID. The sidecar includes the exact coordinate convention and transforms; the CSV alone is not self-describing enough for physical reconstruction. The PNG overlay preserves only the selected view’s rendered appearance (channel mixture, window/LUT, slice/time, opacity, rasterization and alpha); it is not raw data or a full annotation geometry export. The crop preserves original voxel values only for a lossless direct crop and does not preserve the entire source, its vendor-specific metadata, or other planes. Its sidecar records exactly what was retained and dropped. An optional mask overlay may use OME-NGFF `image-label` metadata when axes/scales are representable; that standardized path preserves an integer mask and color metadata, not points/polygons, free-form geometry, or all project history [S1].

## Atomic save, recovery, and portability

Use SQLite transactions for project rows, annotation edits, command log, and export registration. Start with local single-writer database and rollback-journal mode; do not promise crash durability on filesystems that do not honor locking or flush semantics. SQLite’s own explanation lists assumptions and distinguishes WAL [S9]. At startup, recover through SQLite, run integrity checking, and present recoverable errors instead of overwriting a damaged database. Keep a verified rolling backup made through SQLite’s backup mechanism or a closed snapshot. For sharing, close/checkpoint the DB, copy it into a sibling staging bundle, include a manifest with hashes and a `COMPLETE` marker written after file verification, then publish a new bundle/archive on the same tested filesystem. Preserve the previous bundle until the new one is verified. Same-filesystem rename of one staged archive is a candidate publish step, not a guarantee for every SMB/NFS/consumer device. Reopen the published bundle in a clean profile before declaring export success.

Reference bundles are small and avoid duplicating a 3 GB or larger source; they depend on the external file remaining reachable and byte-identical. Portable bundles include copied originals and can grow by the size of those sources, consume storage, and take time to hash/copy. Offer per-image “include original” selection and clearly show estimated size and copy progress. In either mode the bundle includes project database, transform/provenance records, annotations, view state, styles, and selected derivatives. Shared storage is an exchange medium, not live multiuser editing: enforce one writer per local working copy; concurrent edits can be reconciled only as explicit competing project copies in this first version.

## Bounded support and failure behavior

**Initial candidate support matrix (proposed, not yet qualified):**

- Local OME-Zarr 0.4 / Zarr v2, 2–5 named axes with understood `t`, `c`, `z`, `y`, `x`, scalar per-voxel `uint8`, `uint16`, `int16`, or `float32`, finite positive supported sampling, and no unsupported transforms.
- Single-file OME-TIFF using a supported `tifffile` series/OME-XML mapping and tested codec/sample combinations; common two-dimensional images or `Z/C/T` series mapped explicitly. Start with uncompressed plus selected tested Deflate/LZW variants only if the exact pinned decoder supports them. Multi-file OME-TIFF, especially pyramidal sets, is outside the first boundary until fixtures prove it.
- Other TIFF and vendor formats are not accepted by extension alone. A selected simple grayscale 2D TIFF may be added only after its axes/calibration policy is explicit. No automatic assumption that arbitrary TIFF pages mean `Z`.

Unsupported or ambiguous input produces a non-destructive import report: captured metadata and source identity remain in the project; no partially registered usable image is created; the user may choose a supported series, provide an axis order/unit/origin correction with a preview, retry, or cancel. Unreadable files and unknown compression leave the source untouched and the project intact. An incomplete hash/copy remains in a staging state, never an accepted asset. Preserve rejected import diagnostics so a later reader plugin can address the case.

## Consequential public failure/fix/test chain

napari issue [#6068](https://github.com/napari/napari/issues/6068) reports that a 3D multiscale Labels layer rendered the coarsest level but ray-based mouse-value sampling used unreduced coordinates and raised an out-of-range `IndexError`. The reported fixture was a 128³ layer with a 128×64×64 lower-resolution level [S5]. The linked fix [PR #6317](https://github.com/napari/napari/pull/6317), merged as `e2baaa7b3ff8a0bd7d6e3c8db5ab39ff6746bb7d`, sets the data level for 3D, scales ray endpoints for downsampling, computes a bounding box at the chosen level, and adds `test_3D_multiscale_labels_in_3D` assertions with expected ray-sampled values [S6, S7]. napari 0.4.19’s release notes explicitly include “Bugfix: Account for multiscale for labels in 3d” and link PR #6317, so this chain is applicable no later than that release [S8].

The lesson for this plan is consequential: viewer level selection, mouse readout, and annotation coordinate mapping must all use the same data level and invertible mapping. Test pointer-to-voxel values as well as visible overlays at every pyramid level. The chain’s limit is equally important: the regression tests use synthetic 2× multiscale label arrays and a specific 3D sampling path. They do not qualify arbitrary physical origins, anisotropic/oblique axes, OME-Zarr interoperability, exported geometry, nor every GPU/render path. A separate open napari issue [#6320](https://github.com/napari/napari/issues/6320) reports a center-versus-corner shift when switching between pyramid resolutions [S11]. It is not closed in the captured current issue record, and no fix/test chain is claimed here. The chosen center convention and corner/center fixtures below are therefore release blockers, not settled facts.

## Useful opportunities, alternatives, and deferred work

The OME-NGFF opportunity is chunk-addressable local arrays with explicit axes, units, and per-scale transforms; later this same reader boundary may support a shared read-only network/object store without changing the annotation model [S1]. Do not add cloud services or collaboration protocols to the first release. A good export opportunity is integer segmentation-mask interchange via NGFF `image-label`, with the shape/table sidecar retained for richer geometry [S1].

A plausible broader-reader alternative is a Bio-Formats adapter. Its v7.3.1 supported-format documentation lists many acquisition formats and exposes per-format support ratings, which is a concrete lead for mapping the group’s actual instruments to a reader backlog [S10]. This could address CZI, ND2, LIF, and other files without implementing proprietary parsers, but format-specific metadata/sample/calibration behavior still needs testing and a JVM/runtime/packaging choice. Do not advertise the full list as supported. Add an adapter only for a named group instrument, test its exact reader version against vendor-produced fixtures, and preserve the original file and original metadata. Automatic segmentation, registration, simultaneous editing, cloud storage, and broad format imports are optional future work, not minimum obligations.

## Proposed validation gates (all UNEXECUTED unless identified in witnesses)

1. **Reader and support matrix:** build synthetic and instrument-consented fixtures for 2D and `t/c/z/y/x`, integer and floating samples, OME-TIFF `DimensionOrder` permutations and explicit `TiffData`, supported compressed/uncompressed strips/tiles, BigTIFF if admitted, and NGFF 0.4 chunks/pyramid. Compare selected planes/regions and metadata to independent ground truth; confirm unsupported metadata is retained and import can be canceled. Tifffile component examples are not this test.
2. **3 GB bounded access:** on the target 8-core/16 GB workstation, open a 3 GB source without a full decoded copy; record memory peak, cold/warm viewport latency, chunk cache occupancy/eviction, time and progress for metadata/hash scan, slice/time/channel navigation, and cancellation latency. Exercise tiled and strip storage and compressed codecs. Define response thresholds with researchers before release; GPU-off is mandatory test condition. No result is claimed here.
3. **Coordinates and multiscales:** use an asymmetric small volume with unique sample values, named axis permutation, anisotropic scale, nonzero translation, channel/time selectors, and at least three pyramid levels. Round-trip screen→data→physical→data, test half-pixel centers and voxel corners, overlay point/polygon/mask on each level, and exercise volume/all-time scopes. Include issue #6068’s 3D value sampling behavior and issue #6320’s center/corner case. Fail import or export on singular/inconsistent mappings.
4. **Display versus pixels and transforms:** hash source before/after window/level, channel mix, LUT, and gamma operations. Verify the raw sample array is unchanged. Then separately crop/resample, assert output sample values/dtype and transform, record interpolation/parameters/operation ID, and verify annotations stay on the source unless an explicit annotation-transform command is invoked.
5. **Save/recovery/share:** inject process termination during annotation transaction, import copy, DB snapshot, ZIP creation, and publish; reopen and ensure either previous committed state or full new state is present. Test `PRAGMA integrity_check`, backup/restore, duplicate bundle open, relocation with matching and changed hash, and clean-profile reopen on a second OS/workstation. Run on each filesystem used by the group; no universal atomic-rename or network-lock assumption.
6. **Exports/accessibility:** independently read CSV+sidecar, overlay, and crop; verify image SHA-256, coordinate convention, mask IDs, written dtype, crop origin, view flattening details, and omitted metadata statement. Keyboard-only import/navigation/annotate/edit/undo/redo/save/export, focus visibility, screen-reader labels where available, and legible errors are acceptance checks. Verify undo/redo after closing and reopening.

## First delivery increments

**Increment 1:** decide schema and axis/coordinate vocabulary; collect unaltered representative files and expected metadata; implement read-only metadata inspection and explicit unsupported reports.  
**Increment 2:** implement region-backed image access, worker cancellation/cache telemetry, channel/window/level and z/t navigation; pass bounded-memory and axis fixtures.  
**Increment 3:** integrate the chosen canvas with persisted point/polygon/mask records, named selectors, style presets, and transactional command history.  
**Increment 4:** implement local recovery, relocation/hash verification, bundle staging, table/overlay/crop export and clean-profile reopen.  
**Increment 5:** qualify OS/filesystem/package combinations and only then publish a user-facing supported-format list. Keep any adapter or feature outside these gates clearly labeled optional.

## Discovery method and coverage

This research reserved roughly 30% of its discovery plan for mechanisms beyond a whole-product viewer: published format and axis standards, a TIFF/Zarr selected-read implementation, SQLite transaction/recovery mechanics, label-mask interchange, and an alternative reader inventory. No exact elapsed-time partition was recorded, so no measured 30% claim is made. The discovered leads and verified applicability limits are in `leads.json`; exact public captures and their pins are in `sources.json`.